.TH EUCLID-FTP 8 "September 2026" "euclid 1.0.53" "Euclid System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-ftp \- Euclid FTP service process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-ftp
.BR \-\-transfer\-server | \-t
.I ID
[\fB\-\-config\fR|\fB\-c\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-socket\fR|\fB\-s\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR|\fB\-l\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
[\fB\-\-console\-log\fR \fIBOOL\fR]
[\fB\-\-file\-log\fR \fIBOOL\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B euclid-ftp
serves one FTP transfer server: it listens on that server's port,
authenticates clients against
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1)
credentials, and stores whatever they upload as objects in the server's
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1)
bucket.
.PP
It is not normally started by hand. The Euclid manager
.RB ( euclid-mgr )
runs exactly the transfer servers whose definitions are marked running,
so the way to start and stop one is
.BR euclid-cli-ets-start-server (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-ets-stop-server (1);
the manager spawns and reaps this process to match. Starting it directly
is useful for debugging a definition, and for reading an error that the
manager would otherwise only report as a service that did not become
ready.
.PP
The bucket is what a client actually sees. A directory listing is a
listing of keys, an upload becomes an object, and a download reads one
back \- so two servers pointed at the same bucket show the same
contents, as does anything put there through ESM, an SDK or another
transfer server. Local files under the spool directory exist only for as
long as a transfer is in flight.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-transfer\-server " " \fIID\fR
Serve the ETS transfer server with this ID. Required: address, port,
passive port range, bucket and the users and groups allowed to log in
all come from that definition, and there is nothing to serve without
one. A definition is created with
.BR euclid-cli-ets-create-server (1).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
JSON configuration file. Defaults to
.IR /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid.json .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-socket " " \fIPATH\fR
Unix domain socket this process listens on for its own control requests.
Defaults to
.IR /var/run/euclid-ftp.sock .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-console\-log " " \fIBOOL\fR
Log to standard output. Defaults to \fItrue\fR, which is what puts the
log into the manager's journal when it runs this process.
.TP
.BR \-\-file\-log " " \fIBOOL\fR
Log to a file. Defaults to \fIfalse\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The transfer server's definition decides everything that distinguishes
one server from another, and overrides the configuration file where they
overlap \- the definition is the record the manager started this process
from, and letting a stale configuration key win would make the two
disagree. Only installation-wide settings are read here:
.TP
.I euclid.modules.ftp.advertised\-address
Address given to clients in the
.B PASV
response. This describes the host's own NAT situation rather than any
one server's, which is why it is not part of a definition. Empty means
the address the control connection arrived on.
.TP
.I euclid.modules.ftp.data\-dir
Root of the spool directory. Each server gets its own subdirectory,
.IR transfer\- ID,
used for files in flight. Defaults to
.IR /usr/local/euclid/data/ftp .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The process stopped normally.
.TP
.B 1
Startup failed and the reason is on standard error: no
.BR \-\-transfer\-server ,
a server ID that is not defined, a definition with no bucket or an
invalid address, a port that could not be bound, or a spool directory
that could not be created.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid.json
Default configuration file.
.TP
.I /usr/local/euclid/data/ftp/transfer-\fIID\fP
Spool directory for one transfer server's in-flight files.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a defined server in the foreground to see why it will not start:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-ftp --transfer-server ftp1 --loglevel debug
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
